--TEST--
A subclass constructor that skips `parent::constructor(...)` on one path is refused, while every path calling it and a parent with no constructor are both fine — rule:classes/definite-property-initialization
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A parent's own properties are assigned in the parent's constructor, so a
// subclass discharges them by calling it. The call is trusted rather than
// re-derived, which is why one path missing it is the whole refusal.
class Account {
    public function constructor(public string $owner) {}
}

// Refused: the path where `$sponsored` is false leaves `Account::$owner` with
// nothing in it.
class Trial extends Account {
    public function constructor(string $owner, public bool $sponsored) {
        if ($sponsored) {
            parent::constructor($owner);
        }
    }
}

// Legal, and the other side of the same bound: both arms call it, so every
// path through this constructor discharges the parent.
class Paid extends Account {
    public function constructor(string $owner, public int $cents) {
        if ($cents > 0) {
            parent::constructor($owner);
        } else {
            parent::constructor("unbilled");
        }
    }
}

// Legal: a parent that declares no constructor demands nothing of a subclass
// that declares one.
class Plain {}

class Counted extends Plain {
    public function constructor(public int $n) {}
}
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0410]: `Trial` extends another class, but a path through its constructor never calls `parent::constructor(...)`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
